  { q:"How accurate is Mifflin-St Jeor?", a:"Research shows 85–90% accuracy versus measured metabolic rate, better than Harris-Benedict. Individual variation of 10–15% is normal." },
  { q:"What does BMR measure?", a:"BMR measures calories burned at complete rest for basic bodily functions: breathing, circulation, cell repair, brain function." },
  { q:"How to measure BMR accurately?", a:"Best methods: indirect calorimetry in lab, BMR calculator using Mifflin-St Jeor for everyday use, or track weight changes to calibrate." },
  { q:"Who developed the Mifflin-St Jeor equation?", a:"Dr. Mifflin and Dr. St Jeor published the equation in 1990 based on a study of 247 healthy adults. It replaced the older Harris-Benedict equation with a more accurate model for modern populations." },
  { q:"Can Mifflin-St Jeor be used for TDEE?", a:"Yes. Mifflin-St Jeor gives you BMR, which you then multiply by an activity factor (1.2 to 1.9) to get TDEE. Our <a href='/tdee-calculator/'>TDEE calculator</a> automates this." },
  { q:"Does Mifflin-St Jeor work for athletes?", a:"It works well for most people, but very lean or very muscular athletes may need adjustments. Katch-McArdle or Cunningham equations, which use body fat percentage, can be more accurate for this population." },
];
